## 1. Introduction
During the past century, human activities have increased with the development of industrialization and urbanization, and the anthropogenic pollutants from industrial and non-industrial sectors have led to a series of serious water pollution problems [1]. With the increasing of the environmental protection awareness among the general public，water security has been paid broad attention [2]. Moreover, as a kind of important pollutant in water, large organic pollutants such as pharmaceutical, dyes molecule, pesticide have attracted intensive concerns due to the residues of those pollutions in wastewater is a risk for public health and environment [3]. TC is the second most popular antibiotic, which is one of the classic organic pollutants, has been widely used in various aspects such as human and animal drugs over a long period of time without enough attention [4]. However, as a good water soluble antibiotic, TC is hard to be cleanup by microbiological degradation, so that it may be easily store and accumulate in the water environment. Obviously, it is necessary to remove TC from wastewater. Nowadays, various methods have been used for the removal of hazardous large organic pollutants from aqueous solution. Among the common techniques, adsorption has been regarded as one of the most competitive techniques, account for the low cost with a wide range of applications, and it is easy to operate without harmful secondary products [4].
In recent years, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been developed as a new class of crystalline nanoporous materials, which are consisted of metal clusters and organic ligands [5, 6]. The attention on MOFs has increased quickly due to the high surface areas, huge porosity and pore geometry [4, 6, 7]. Furthermore, tailoring the structures and properties of such porous materials would be easy to operate and realize [8]. Accordingly, MOFs are widely studied and applied in many fields, such as gases storage/separation, catalysis, chemical sensor as well as organic pollutants capture in wastewater [9]. However, most of the stable MOF adsorbents like UiO-66 are microporous, which severely slow down the intracrystalline diffusion and mass transfer, limit the application in adsorbing large organic pollutants [10]. Although the pore sizes of MOFs can be changed from microporous to mesoporous scale simultaneity along with the change of the inorganic moiety connectivity and the organic linkers in nature [4] theoretically, the modified MOFs are vulnerable in water and hydrochloric acid [11]. Moreover, the structures of resulting materials may be interpenetrating, this would cause the pore sizes to be much smaller [12]. Therefore, finding a simple and convenient way to solve this problem is essential.
Recently, the distinct kinds of defects introduced into MOFs have gained wide attentions account for the good diffusivity and the active sites [13]. In the previous work, we have proposed an in-situ self-assembly method to synthesize various hierarchical-pore metal-organic framework (H-MOFs) by using metal-organic assemblies as the templates [14]. Herein, on the basis of this strategy, synthesis of H-MOFs has been achieved by reflux condition on liter scale, the resulting materials with different pore sizes exhibit good crystallinity and surface areas, which could be applied to adsorb large organic pollutants.
In the current work, we successfully synthesized two kinds of H-MOFs based on UiO-66 (H-UiO-66s) by reflux on liter scale, which have average mesopore sizes at $3.8~\mathrm{nm}$ and $17.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ respectively. With the purpose of distinguishing easily, these two H-MOFs were designated as H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm, respectively. Considering the existence of large mesopore size of H-UiO-66s, the adsorption experiments were carried out to explore the adsorption capacities of large organic pollutants over the materials. Remarkably, comparing with microporous
UiO-66, both H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm show superior capture performance for TC. Especially, H-UiO-66-17.3 nm represents an excellent adsorption capacity of $666.67~\mathrm{mg{\cdotg}^{-1}}$ , which is much higher than those of various reported adsorbents. Meanwhile, H-UiO-66s also show excellent adsorption capacities for other large organic pollutants such as RhB and TA, the representatives of organic dyes and food additives. These results show that the H-UiO-66s could effectively remove large organic pollutants because the interactions between TC and adsorbents have synergistic reaction with the large pore size of the H-MOFs, indicating that H-UiO-66s may be the potential adsorbents for large organic pollutants removal from wastewater.
## 2. Experiments
## 2.1. Materials
TC $\mathrm{(C_{20}H_{24}N_{2}O_{8})}$ , RhB $\left(\mathbf{C}_{28}\mathbf{H}_{31}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}\mathbf{N}_{2}\mathbf{O}_{3}\right)$ , TA $\mathrm{(C_{76}H_{52}O_{46})}$ , sodium hydroxide (NaOH), hydrochloric acid (HCl) and acetone $\mathrm{(CH_{3}O C H_{3})}$ ) were purchased from Beijing Chemical Works. Benzoic acid (C6H5COOH, HBC) (purity $\geq$ $99\%$ ) and zirconium tetrachloride $\mathrm{(ZrCl_{4})}$ were purchased from Aladdin Industrial Corporation. Zinc nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{26}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ and N,N’-Dimethylformamide (DMF) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd. P-phthalic acid $\mathrm{(C_{8}H_{6}O_{4}}$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC})$ ) (purity $=99\mathrm{~\%}$ ) was purchased from J&K Scientific Ltd. All chemicals were analytical grade regents at least and used as received.
## 2.2. Preparation of microporous UiO-66
## 2.3. Preparation of H-UiO-66s
Two kinds of H-UiO-66s were prepared by heat reflux method at liter scale based on our previous work [15]. H-UiO-66-17.3 nm was synthesized by dissolving $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(6.70\ \mathrm{g})$ and HBC $(54.90\ \mathrm{g})$ in a $1000~\mathrm{{mL}}$ round-bottom flask with 600.00 mL DMF at room temperature. The mixture was then kept in oil bath at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and stirred for 12 h at first. $\mathrm{ZrCl_{4}}$ $(3.60~\mathrm{g})$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\left(4.80\ \mathrm{g}\right)$ were subsequently added and the mixture were heated at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ in the round-bottom flask. After the flask was gradually cooled down to room temperature, the resulting sample was obtained by centrifugation, and washed three times with DMF to extract the non-reacted $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ firstly. The solid was then washed three times by weak acid DMF to destroy the template, after that, the as-synthesized product was washed three times with DMF to remove the decomposed template residues and make the product neutral. Finally, in order to further remove the DMF, the particles were washed with acetone repeatedly. The final sample was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$ in vacuum oven, respectively. H-UiO-66-3.8 nm was prepared with the same method instead of the mass of $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ was $4.46~\mathrm{g}$ .
## 2.4. Characterization and analytical methods
Autosorb-IQ-MP BET surface area analyzer was used to measure the specific surface area, total pore volume as well as pore size distribution by nitrogen adsorption-desorption method at $77~\mathrm{K}$ -
The powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) patterns were determined by using D8 Advance powder diffractometer machine, the 2θ of which was ranging from $3^{\circ}$ to $50~^{\circ}$ with a step size of 0.02 °. Nicolet 6700 fourier transform infrared spectrometer was used to record the FTIR spectra which the recording range of it was $2000{-}400~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ . The thermal gravimetric analysis (TGA) data were collected by TGA Q50 thermogravimetric analysis of Waters Corp in nitrogen atmosphere, the data were measured from $40~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with the heating rate of 5 $^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ /min as well as the nitrogen flow rate of $10~\mathrm{ml/min}$ . TU-1901 UV-vis spectrophotometer of Beijing Purkinje General Instrument Co., LTD. was used to determine the UV-vis absorption spectra. X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) spectral was used to measure the surface elemental composition by ESCALAB 250 X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy.
## 2.5. Adsorption experiments
All the aqueous solutions were prepared by dissolving solid adsorbates in distilled deionized water. The MOF adsorbents $(10.00~\mathrm{mg})$ were put into TC ( $\mathrm{10.00~mL}$ solution with desired concentrations in glass bottles with caps, respectively. These bottles were placed in the constant temperature shaker at the temperature of 303 K for predetermined time. After adsorption experiments, the slurries were filtered, and the TC solution concentrations were examined by UV-vis spectrometer. To estimate the effect of pH, 0.1M NaOH and 0.1M HCl were used to adjust the pH values throughout the adsorption experiments. All the batch experiments were repeated three times. The adsorption isotherms of RhB and TA were performed with the similar adsorption procedures of TC.
The adsorption capacity was calculated by the equation as:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{q_{e}=\frac{(C_{0}-C_{e})V}{M}}\end{array}
$$
where ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is the uptake of MOF at equilibrium, ${\bf C}_{0}(\mathrm{mg}\cdot\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ is the initial concentration of organic pollutant solution, $\mathbf{C}_{\mathrm{e}}(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{L}^{-1})$ is the equilibrium concentration of the solution, V (L) is the volume of the solution which is used in the experiment, M (g) is the dosage of adsorbent.
## 2.6. Regeneration methods
H-UiO-66-17.3 nm with adsorbed TC has been taken as an example to achieve the regeneration. The TC loaded material was eluted by 0.1 M HCl aqueous solution at stirred condition for 12 hours, and the regenerated H-MOF was used for TC adsorption for four cycles.
## 3. Results and discussion
In view of the large mesopore structures of H-UiO-66s, the materials are supposed to be the outstanding adsorbents for removing large organic pollutants from wastewater. Before the adsorption experiments, various characterizations were performed to testify the structure of H-UiO-66s. Firstly, the PXRD was employed to characterize the structures of the H-UiO-66s and the results were illustrated in Fig. 1. Obviously, the PXRD patterns of H-UiO-66s are similar with that of microporous UiO-66, which confirm the good crystallinity of H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm.
To further verify the mesoporous structures of H-UiO-66s synthesized at reflux condition, $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms at $77~\mathrm{K}$ were performed. As shown in Fig. 2 (a) and (b), both H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm show representative type Ⅳ $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms and the obvious hysteresis loop are observed, which indicate the existence of mesoporous structures. The pore size distributions of the H-UiO-66s were displayed in Fig. 2 (c) and (d), the mesopore diameters of H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm are $3.8\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $17.3\ \mathrm{nm}$ ， respectively, depending on the amount of template precursors used in synthesis process. As a contrast, the $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm of UiO-66 (Fig. S1) exhibits to be the typeⅠ, indicating defect-free structure UiO-66 has been synthesized by the template-free method. In addition, the specific surface area of UiO-66, H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm are 1204.00, 1168.10 and $1113.04\ \mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively, as shown in Table 1. However, the total pore volumes $\mathrm{(V_{t})}$ of H-UiO-66s are both much higher than that of microporous UiO-66, especially H-UiO-66 17.3 nm $(1.06\mathrm{cc/g})$ , which is nearly twice larger than that of UiO-66 $(0.59\mathrm{cc/g})$ , indicating the existence of mesoporous structures in H-UiO-66s can efficiently enhance the total pore volume of UiO-66. Meanwhile, the micropore volumes $(\mathrm{V_{micro}})$ in H-UiO-66s are much smaller than that in microporous UiO-66, which imply the introduction of defects influenced both the microporosity and the mesoporosity of MOFs.
