Fig. S2 illustrated the FTIR spectra of three MOFs, the FTIR spectra of H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and
H-UiO-66-17.3 nm are similar to the spectrum of UiO-66, which indicate that the mesoporous H-UiO-66s have the same chemical constituents as those of microporous UiO-66. To verify the stability of H-UiO-66, TGA was performed, as shown in Fig. S3. It can be seen that H-UiO-66s have excellent thermal stability and the frameworks start to decompose at the temperature of $350^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in nitrogen, which are similar to that of the microporous UiO-66. The final TGA profiles confirm the fact that the defects introduced into H-UiO-66s have no obvious impact on the thermal stability of H-UiO-66s.
The above results show the successful synthesis of H-UiO-66s, which encourage us to test the performance of H-UiO-66s for large organic pollutants capture. Herein, we selected TC as an example to perform a series of adsorption experiments to investigate adsorption capabilities of H-UiO-66s.
Firstly, adsorption experiments were performed by soaking three kinds of MOF adsorbents in TC solution with the same initial concentration for sufficient time, as shown in Fig. 3 (a). For H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm, the aqueous solutions become nearly colorless, which imply TC is efficiently adsorbed in H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm. However, no obvious color changes of the TC solution is observed on the case of the microporous UiO-66. The results are further confirmed by UV-vis spectra of TC solution before and after adsorption by the three MOFs (Fig. 3 (b)). After adsorption, the absorbance curve of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm display an apparent decline compared with that of microporous UiO-66. It also can be seen that the MOF samples after adsorption turn various shades of yellow-orange (the color of TC), especially H-UiO-66-17.3 nm powder (Fig. 3 (c)). The above phenomena suggest that H-UiO-66-17.3 nm
exhibits a best adsorption performance on TC.
The $\mathrm{pH}$ is one of the most important factors to understand the adsorption process of TC, and the adsorption process can also be affected by different pH values due to the fact that TC molecules can present diverse ionic conditions by protonation or/and deprotonation in these situations [16]. To search an optimum condition for TC capture, the adsorption experiments were performed at various pH values from 2 to 8 prior to the addition of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm. As illustrated in Fig. S4, the adsorbed quantity of TC over H-UiO-66-17.3 nm increase with increasing pH in the range of 2-5, then decrease as the pH further varies from 5 to 7. Considering of the highest TC uptake capacity, all the further adsorption experiments were performed at the pH value of 5.
