As shown in Fig. 4, because of the available surface area for adsorption, the adsorbed quantities of TC in H-UiO-66s increase rapidly during the first $2\mathrm{~h~}$ . After the rapid uptake stage, although the uptake rate decline, the adsorbed quantities continue to grow with increasing time until to reach the equilibrium quantities within $12\mathrm{~h~}$ approximately. The reduced adsorption rate is for the reason that the gradual exhaustion of the adsorption capacities of these three adsorbents, which leads to the adsorption sites of TC molecules transport from external of the adsorbents to internal pores [18]. In addition, H-UiO-66-17.3 nm can remove $85\%$ of TC within the first $2\mathrm{h}$ and the removal efficiency can reach more than $99\%$ after equilibrium. However, microporous UiO-66 exhibits the low removal efficiency with only $17\%$ for TC even after adsorption equilibrium. These results agreed well with those observed in Fig. 3.
To further study the adsorption kinetics charateristic, two most commonly used kinetic models, pseudo-first-order kinetic model (2) and pseudo-second-order kinetic model (3) were applied to describe the adsorption kinetic of TC onto H-UiO-66-3.8 nm and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm, which were shown in Fig. S5.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{\ln(q_{e}-q_{t})=\ln q_{e}-k_{1}\mathrm{t}}}\\ {{\displaystyle{\frac{t}{q_{t}}=\frac{t}{q_{e}}+\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}^{2}}}}}\end{array}
$$
where t (h) is the adsorption time, $\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}{\cdot}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ is the adsorbed amount of the adsorbate at time t, $\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}$ $(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is the uptake of MOF at equilibrium, $\mathbf{k}_{1}$ is the pseudo-first-order rate constant $(\mathbf{h}^{-1})$ , $\mathbf{k}_{2}$ is the pseudo-second-order rate constant $(\mathrm{g}\cdot\mathrm{mg}^{-1}{\cdot}\mathrm{h}^{-1})$ .
Table S1 summarized the related parameters and correlation coefficients of the two models. As can be seen, pseudo-second-order kinetic model could well describes the adsorption of TC on H-UiO-66-17.3 nm and H-UiO-66-3.8 nm because both the ${\bf R}^{2}$ values of the pseudo-second-order kinetic model are higher than 0.99, being much better than those of the pseudo-first-order kinetic model. Furthermore, kinetic parameters are positively correlated with the pore size of adsorbents, this result is due to the fact that more active adsorption sites expose with the increasing pore size of adsorbents, which accelerate the adsorption processes.
The adsorption isotherms of TC over UiO-66 and H-UiO-66s were employed to assess the adsorption capacities. Considering the sufficient time was needed to reach adsorption equilibrium, the adsorption isotherms of UiO-66 and H-UiO-66s were obtained after adsorption for 48 hours. As illustrated in Fig. 5, the adsorption capacities of three kinds of adsorbents grow with the increasing TC concentration until to achieve the maximum adsorption capacities. The adsorption capacities of TC on H-UiO-66-17.3 nm and H-UiO-66-3.8 nm are $666.67~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ and $487.80\ \mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}}$ , respectively, which are $430\%$ and $288\%$ higher than that of microporous UiO-66 $(125.72~\mathrm{mg\cdotg^{-1}})$ . The results indicate that the adsorbed quantities of TC depended strongly on the diameter of adsorbents, and H-UiO-66-17.3 nm with larger mesopore size has higher adsorption capacity than that of H-UiO-66-3.8 nm, implying larger pore diameter is benefited for TC capture. More importantly, H-UiO-66-17.3 nm owns higher TC adsorption capacity than classic mesopore MOFs such as $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-101(Cr) and MIL-101, as well as some other porous materials, as shown in Table 2. These results suggest that H-UiO-66s are efficient adsorbents for TC capture in wastewater.
In order to well understand the adsorption process, Langmuir isotherm models (4) and Freundlich isotherm models (5) were used to further analyze the equilibrium adsorption characteristics of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm and H-UiO-66-3.8 nm, which were shown in Fig. S6.
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\frac{C_{e}}{q_{e}}=\frac{1}{k_{L}q_{m}}+\frac{C_{e}}{q_{m}}}\end{array}
$$
$$
\begin{array}{r}{\ln{\sf q_{e}}=\ln k_{F}+\frac{1}{\mathrm{n}}\ln{\sf C_{e}}}\end{array}
$$
where ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{m}}({\bf m}{\bf g}{\cdot}{\bf g}^{-1})$ is the maximum adsorbed amount, ${\bf q}_{\mathrm{e}}~(\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ is the uptake of MOF at equilibrium, $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{L}}$ is the Langmuir constant $(\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , $\mathbf{k}_{\mathrm{F}}$ is the Freundlich constant $((\mathrm{L}{\cdot}\mathrm{mg}^{-1})^{1/\mathrm{n}}\mathrm{mg}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ .
It is found that the adsorption data onto H-UiO-66s are in good agreement with Langmuir model, which exhibit the higher ${\bf R}^{2}$ values than those of Freundlich isotherm models. This could be attributed to the defects of H-UiO-66s leading to active adsorption sites distribute homogenously, as Langmuir model is more suitable to the adsorbents with homogenous surface and monolayer adsorption. Table S2 summarized the related parameters and correlation coefficients $(\mathbf{R}^{2})$ of the two models.
To further explore the interactions between TC and adsorbents, XPS analysis was carried out to determine the surface elemental composition of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm before and after adsorption of TC. The wide survey scan spectrum in the range of $\ensuremath{0-800}\ensuremath{\mathrm{eV}}$ was given in Fig. 6 (a), and the peaks of Zr3d, C1s and O1s are observed in the spectrum. Considering the coordinate bonds between the oxygen atom of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}$ and the Zr cluster in H-UiO-66-17.3 nm [28], the attention has been paid to the XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Zr}3\mathrm{d}$ and O1s. As illustrated in Fig. 6 (b), the high resolution Zr3d XPS spectrum of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm was deconvoluted into two individual peaks. Compared with the binding energies of original H-UiO-66-17.3 nm, these two peaks of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm-TC decrease with deviation of $0.3\mathrm{eV}$ and $0.45\mathrm{eV},$ respectively. And the O1s XPS spectrum (Fig. 6 (c)) was also deviates with $0.25\ \mathrm{eV}.$ . It can be inferred from these results that O and $Z\mathbf{r}$ are probably involved into the adsorption progress (the schematic diagram can be seen in Fig. 7 (b)), the surface of TC might incorporate a certain amount of the hydroxyl zirconium oxide groups instead of native hydroxyl [29]. In addition, the benzene ring [30] of H-UiO-66-17.3 nm and the aromatic ring of TC may account for the H-MOF have π-π stacking interaction with TC [27], whose schematic diagram was shown in Fig. 7 (c). The interactions between TC and adsorbents have synergistic reaction with the pore structures of the adsorbents, which are both benefit to the TC adsorption behavior.
